`fetch()` is used to *Consume* or *Retrieve* data from an external site. This data or site is referred to as a web [[API]].

The actual fetch happens on a **Separate Thread** so it is [[Asynchronous JS|asynchronous]]. Think of it like running separately from the rest of your code.

- In JS, Fetch has had universal support since ~2018
- Fetch is still quite new to [[Node.js]], Support since ~2021

`fetch` returns a [[Promise]] object.
```js
console.log("before fetch");
const p = fetch(url); 
```

```js
p.then(             )
```
The parameter for `then` is the **function to be invoked when promise is resolved**
`then` returns another promise, so they can be chained.

```js
p.then(response => { })
```

Example with chaining, which is more common.
```js
fetch(url)
	.then( response => ) 
```
fetch provides the [[HTTP]] response received from the [[URL]]

typically we would use json
```js
fetch(url)
	.then( response => response.json()) 
```
this function will **eventually** extract the [[JSON]] (then returns the promise)
data that is in the response. since the first then returns a promise, we need to chain another then
```js
fetch(url)
	.then(response => response.json()) 
	.then(data => {
				...
			});
```
`data` has a JS object that has been parsed from the received [[JSON]]

It's also common to include a `catch` handler.
```js
fetch(url)
	.then(response => response.json()) 
	.then(data => {
				...
			});
	.catch(error => {  //do something });
```
Unfortunately, the most common error is something like a 404 error with the [[URL]], catch will
only invoke if there is an issue with the [[JSON]] itself. 

```js
fetch(url)
	.then(response => {
						if (response.ok) 
							return response.json();
						else
							throw new Error("Fetch failed");
						});
	.catch(error => {})
```
Note you SHOULD use this!! for for future examples we won't include it

